speechtext:
Right Hon. W. L. MACKENZIE KING (Secretary of State for External Affairs) moved that the house go into committee to consider the following proposed resolution:
That it is expedient that parliament do approve of the convention between Canada and the United States for the preservation of the halibut fishery of the northern Pacific ocean and Bering sea, Ottawa, 9th May, 1930, signed in respect of the Dominion of Canada by the plenipotentiary named therein, and that this house do approve of the same.
He said: In 1923 a treaty was made between the United States and Canada for the purpose of protecting and preserving the halibut fisheries in the northern Pacific ocean and Bering sea. One of the provisions of that treaty was to the effect that an international fisheries commission should be appointed, which commission would have the power to study the life history of the halibut, to report the results of its investigation, and also to make recommendations in the nature of regulations for governing the fishing for halibut in those waters. The international fisheries commission took a period of some three years to make the investigation and reported that the halibut fisheries were being seriously depleted. It made important recommendations for the preservation of the halibut, and these recommendations were approved, so far as Canada is concerned, by order in council in 1928. It was thought that that was all that was necessary to give effect to the recommendations on behalf of Canada. The United States government, however, felt that something more in the way of authority was necessary for the United States to give like effect to the recommendations, and suggested that the former treaty should be modified in a manner which would embody the recommendations. That >s what has been done, and the treaty which f laid on the table a day or two ago, and which the house is now being asked to approve, is virtually the treaty of 1923, as it was but embodying as well the recommendations of the international fisheries commission.
These recommendations are embodied in the following provisions of the treaty:
(a)	divide the convention waters into areas;
(b)	limit the catch of halibut to be taken from each area;
(c)	fix the size and character of halibut fishing appliances to be used therein;
(d)	make such regulations for the collection of statistics of the catch of halibut including the licensing and clearance of vessels, as will
IMr. Mackenzie King.]
enable the International Fisheries Commission to determine the condition and trend of the halibut fishery by banks and areas, as a proper basis for protecting and conserving the fishery;
(e)	close to all halibut fishing such portion or portions of an area or areas, as the International Fisheries Commission find to be populated by small, immature halibut.
